::ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Visual reference: graph one-variable inequalities on a number line with open/closed circles and shading direction.
Solve x + 6 < 10.
{
~A. x < 16#This adds 6 instead of subtracting 6.
=B. x < 4#Correct. Subtract 6 from both sides.
~C. x > 4#The direction does not change when subtracting 6.
~D. x > 16#This combines the wrong operation with the wrong direction.
}

::ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Visual reference: graph one-variable inequalities on a number line with open/closed circles and shading direction.
Solve 2x - 5 >= 7.
{
~A. x >= 1#This subtracts 5 from 7 instead of adding 5 to both sides.
~B. x <= 6#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 2.
~C. x >= 12#This stops before dividing by 2.
=D. x >= 6#Correct. Add 5 to get 2x >= 12, then divide by 2.
}

::ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Visual reference: graph one-variable inequalities on a number line with open/closed circles and shading direction.
Solve -3x <= 12.
{
=A. x >= -4#Correct. Divide by -3 and reverse the inequality symbol.
~B. x <= -4#This finds the boundary but forgets to reverse the symbol.
~C. x >= 4#The sign of the boundary value is wrong.
~D. x <= 4#This has the wrong boundary sign and the wrong direction.
}

::ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Visual reference: graph one-variable inequalities on a number line with open/closed circles and shading direction.
Solve -1 < x + 2 <= 5.
{
~A. 1 < x <= 7#This adds 2 instead of subtracting 2 from all three parts.
~B. -3 <= x < 3#This reverses which endpoints are included.
=C. -3 < x <= 3#Correct. Subtract 2 from each part of the compound inequality.
~D. x < -3 or x >= 3#This changes an interval into an or statement.
}

::ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Visual reference: graph one-variable inequalities on a number line with open/closed circles and shading direction.
Why does the inequality symbol reverse when solving -4x > 20?
{
~A. Because 20 is positive#The sign of 20 is not what causes the reversal.
=B. Because both sides are divided by a negative number#Correct. Multiplying or dividing by a negative reverses the order relationship.
~C. Because x is a variable#Variables do not automatically change inequality direction.
~D. Because the solution is negative#The direction changes because of the negative divisor, not because the answer is negative.
}

